1
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:09,890
Every observation begins as an experience.

2
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:20,273
Perception joins sensory input and interpretation.

3
00:00:21,373 --> 00:00:27,998
The sensory apparatus is not the whole story.

4
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:35,532
[DOT proposes] You are Little c.

5
00:00:36,632 --> 00:00:41,693
[DOT proposes] The Avatar is your living body.

6
00:00:45,493 --> 00:00:53,048
[The prevailing view] “You’re nothing but a pack of neurons.” — Francis Crick, The Astonishing Hypothesis, 1994

7
00:00:54,148 --> 00:01:00,550
Does measuring activity explain experience?

8
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:10,887
We inherit explanations before examining them.

9
00:01:11,987 --> 00:01:19,393
Others fill the gap: markets, platforms, ideologies.

10
00:01:21,793 --> 00:01:28,307
The body protests: stress, anxiety, burnout.

11
00:01:29,407 --> 00:01:36,367
Relief matters. So do the underlying conditions.

12
00:01:37,467 --> 00:01:43,757
Pressure can deepen division and conflict.

13
00:01:44,857 --> 00:01:50,812
At stake: how we understand experience.

14
00:01:54,612 --> 00:01:59,227
Notice who is experiencing.

15
00:02:00,327 --> 00:02:06,170
Test this against your own experience.

16
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:26,403
Digital Organism Theory

17
00:02:26,403 --> 00:02:36,000
Digital Organism Theory
A school of thought for examining experience.
